      "expected_artifact": "Headless probe logs for all four modes (positive, negative, mechanics, recovery) from the accepted candidate commit; a native OS-input playtest bundle with a deliberate-mistake recovery under /company/outputs/exp11/playtests/ (input trace, host+client logs, screenshots, result); and a plain change summary with known limits recorded with the evidence",
      "goal_id": null,
      "id": "11554293-9030-457d-88c0-43397d341be4",
      "integration_branch": null,
      "outcome": "The founder's two reports - sideways movement feeling reversed and the solid-looking truck not stopping anyone - share one root: the prototype has no physics at all. The player is moved numerically on fixed truck axes while the camera is locked facing the cab, and nothing in the world has a collision shape. This work makes first-person movement follow the view with real mouse look, gives the truck and world real collision the player can walk in, and guarantees a dropped crate can always be picked back up, so an ordinary mistake can never soft-lock the delivery.\n\nExact contract. Repo slug swift-arrival (checked out at /company/projects/swift-arrival), from baseline commit 84ff1745b29267708599e94036ec6f7a2a7e0457 (tree 25733ead6eb7c83221048d323838a5cadc2a235e). Preserve the original placeholder assets and labels, the bounded truck route (z 0..40) and destination zone, the ENet host-authoritative model, and the scripted-probe discipline. No content broadening, no third-party assets, no external effects.\n\nDiagnosis to build against, verified in baseline main.gd:\n- Strafe inversion and no look: _apply_player_move adds raw truck-local Vector3(mv.x,0,mv.y) while the camera always faces truck-local +Z, so D moves toward camera-left; there is no mouse look at all.\n- Clamp-only collision: every object is a MeshInstance3D; the player is clamped to the truck interior (x -1..1, z -3.3..3.3) with one special-cased doorway rule; the player can never leave the truck; the truck drives through a dropped crate.\n- Unrecoverable drop: _request_release teleports the crate to world z = truck_z - 5 no matter where the player stands. Hands cannot reach below truck_z - 2.85 and the grab limit is 1.6 m, so a release at route start (truck_z = 0, and the truck cannot reverse below 0) is permanently unrecoverable. This is the owner-observed intermittent failure.\n\nRequired result, one candidate commit:\n1. View-relative first-person movement: strafe and forward follow the camera basis; mouse-look yaw rotates the view and the movement basis; turning while blocked stays coherent (no wall pass-through while turning).\n2. Real collision boundaries replacing clamps: solid truck and world geometry blocks the player from both sides (for example CharacterBody3D move_and_slide against StaticBody3D), the player can leave the truck and walk the bounded area on the ground, the crate is a physical object that rests on geometry, and the truck cannot drive through a dropped crate.\n3. Recoverable crate handling: release places the crate relative to the player's actual position and facing, and every dropped crate in the bounded area remains pick-up-able, including behind the truck at route start; no reachable state requires a restart.\n4. Host-authoritative shared player, vehicle, cargo and delivery state: clients request, the host validates and resolves (including player movement validity, not just interactions), and joining clients receive coherent state.\n5. Legible feedback for pickup, drop, seat and unload: distinct visible cues plus the HUD log; rejections (grab refused, seat occupied) are visible to the requester.\n6. Honest presentation: controls shown match reality; nothing is labeled physical that is not.\n\nProbe contract - probes assert observed state and exit nonzero when the mechanic is broken; a printed PASS without an assertion is not acceptance. These four commands are the acceptance gates, declared atomically with this Work and run in the Attempt workspace:\n- sh run-probe.sh HOSTLOG CLIENTLOG - positive delivery loop green end-to-end (join, pickup, seat, drive bounded route, unload in zone, host-authoritative completion visible on both, clean shutdown).\n- sh run-probe.sh --negative HOSTLOG CLIENTLOG - release outside the zone is rejected by the host, delivery does not complete, both processes log PASS (the baseline host-side negative shutdown was declared not acceptance evidence; finish it).\n- sh run-probe.sh --mechanics HOSTLOG CLIENTLOG - asserts the movement basis follows view yaw (at a set yaw, strafe displaces the player along camera right, not a fixed world axis) and that closed solid geometry blocks movement (near-zero displacement into a wall).\n- sh run-probe.sh --recovery HOSTLOG CLIENTLOG - asserts a crate released behind the truck at route start is recovered and the delivery still completes.\n\nEvidence to preserve and link as artifacts:\n- Headless logs of all four probe modes from the accepted candidate.\n- One native OS-input playtest bundle under /company/outputs/exp11/playtests/RUNID/ (input trace, host and client logs, screenshots, result) including at least one deliberate mistake and its recovery. Native feel evidence is material for lead judgement, not a gate.\n- A plain summary of what changed, what was verified, and known limits; say plainly what was not done. Keep it compact: one focused candidate beats many passes; about 50 USD remains for the whole slice.",
